      "summary": "Introducing the Layers of the Digital Domain | Digital Transformation Architect Embracing Digital Home Guidance Briefings (EDTW) What changed Interviews (EDT) How others did it Architectural Deep Dives What to do next Learn Digital Transformation Overview Pillars AI, Cyber, Data, Edge, Computing, Comms ODXA Domains &amp; mappings Whitepapers Monthly canonical synthesis Articles Explainers &amp; analysis Lectures (DTA) Learning series Community Community Newsletter Patreon LinkedIn About About Host Sponsors Shop Search العربية Deutsch English Français Italiano Português 日本語 Introducing the Layers of the Digital Domain 2026-06-10 by Dr. Darren Pulsipher Download PDF Modernization often starts with a familiar promise: new platforms, cleaner integrations, better data, faster delivery. But in many organizations, the work stalls anyway. The reason is not always a lack of effort or talent. More often, the Digital Domain is being treated as an execution space instead of an architectural one. That distinction matters. If leaders carefully design the strategy, process, and organizational sides of change, but leave the digital side as an undifferentiated pile of systems and tasks, the overall transformation becomes fragile. Digital systems belong inside the architecture model, not outside it as mere IT execution. When that is forgotten, modernization tends to become fragmented, duplicate work appears, and responsibility becomes blurred. This lecture introduces the Digital Domain as a layered architectural model. That is the core idea: the Digital Domain should not be described only by what teams build or operate. It should be understood through its structure, because structure explains how digital capability is composed, governed, and changed over time. Why the Digital Domain Needs an Architectural Definition Many people think of the Digital Domain as “where the technology lives.” That is understandable, because this is where software, integration, and data actually reside. Bu",
