      "summary": "Editorial Intelligence | Embracing Digital Embracing Digital Home Intelligence Briefings Interviews Editorial intelligence · Updated 2026-10-09 Signals to trends to better decisions A small, reviewed set of evolving technology signals and the implications they may have for people, process, and technology. Active trend Artificial intelligence Editorially reviewed Building AI governance is moving into execution AI governance is shifting from principles and announcements toward operating controls, accountability, and redesigned work. The current evidence points to a practical transition: leaders are responding to oversight, bias and accountability concerns while testing how AI changes real workflows. The evidence is early and does not establish a universal adoption pattern. Evidence The October 5 Embracing Digital This Week briefing identifies tighter oversight, scrutiny of bias and accountability, and responsible AI management as active leadership concerns. The same briefing reports that organizations are beginning to test AI augmentation in real workflows, connecting governance to operating change rather than policy alone. Counterevidence and limits The available pilot evidence is editorial and limited to the linked briefing and deep dive. It does not quantify adoption across industries or establish causality. Watch for next Named owners and review mechanisms attached to production AI use cases Governance controls appearing in workflow and product decisions Evidence that AI augmentation changes work design, not only task automation Signal Reviewed · 2026-10-05 Leadership attention is moving toward accountable AI operations EDW-187 describes governance, bias, accountability, and early AI-augmentation experiments as connected issues for digital-transformation leaders. EDW-187 briefing → Analysis What changes when AI governance becomes operating practice? The practical question is no longer whether an organization has an AI principle. It is whether that principle change",
