      "summary": "Embracing Digital Transformation Embracing Digital Home Guidance Briefings (EDTW) What changed Interviews (EDT) How others did it Architectural Deep Dives What to do next Learn Digital Transformation Overview Pillars AI, Cyber, Data, Edge, Computing, Comms ODXA Domains &amp; mappings Whitepapers Monthly canonical synthesis Articles Explainers &amp; analysis Lectures (DTA) Learning series Intelligence Community Community Newsletter Patreon LinkedIn About About Host Sponsors Shop Search Skip to content Home Episodes Episode 20: &lt;$- name Episode 20 &lt;$- name 2026-06-03 Hosted by Dr. Darren Pulsipher Explore more in the episode archive . Summary Most organizations do not fail at digital transformation because they lack ideas. They fail because the ideas do not survive contact with day-to-day work. A new capability is announced, a pilot begins, the diagram looks promising, and then reality gets in the way: handoffs are unclear, approvals are inconsistent, ownership is fuzzy, and the work that matters most is still done by habit rather than by design. That gap between intention and execution is the problem the FORGE practices are meant to address. Darren W Pulsipher Most organizations do not fail at digital transformation because they lack ideas. They fail because the ideas do not survive contact with day-to-day work. A new capability is announced, a pilot begins, the diagram looks promising, and then reality gets in the way: handoffs are unclear, approvals are inconsistent, ownership is fuzzy, and the work that matters most is still done by habit rather than by design. That gap between intention and execution is the problem the FORGE practices are meant to address. Copyright &copy; Paidar Productions LLC All rights reserved",
